{{NZelectorateShortDesc|type=general|current=yes|region=southland}}
'''Invercargill''' is an electorate of the [[New Zealand Parliament]] that has existed since 1866. Since the {{NZ election link|2020}}, the electorate's representative is [[Penny Simmonds]] of the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]].

==Population centres==
{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Invercargill.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
The electorate covers [[Invercargill]] city and the surrounding rural communities, including [[Bluff, New Zealand|Bluff]], [[Riverton / Aparima]], [[Wallacetown]] and [[Stewart Island / Rakiura]].<ref name="parllib">{{cite web| url= https://www3.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/electorate-profiles/invercargill-electorate-profile/ |title=Invercargill - Electorate Profile |publisher= Parliamentary Library |date= 27 April 2021 |access-date= 22 September 2025}}</ref> In 1996 a boundary redistribution resulted in the abolition of the [[Awarua (New Zealand electorate)|Awarua electorate]] and merged with Invercargill following re-drawing of boundaries due to the introduction of mixed-member proportional voting ([[Mixed-member proportional representation|MMP]]). Minor but steady population decline in the Southland region has generally resulted in Invercargill expanding northwards. The 2013 redistribution, however, has left Invercargill unchanged.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2014 |url=http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=26 September 2014 |page=10 |date=4 April 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006075102/http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |archive-date=6 October 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref> The 2020 redistribution added a large area around [[Clifden, New Zealand|Clifden]] and [[Tuatapere]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://vote.nz/assets/Boundary-Review/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2020.pdf |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2020 |date=17 April 2020}}</ref> The 2025 boundary review saw the communities of [[Heddon Bush]], [[Drummond, New Zealand|Drummond]] and [[Isla Bank]] transferred to the electorate from {{NZ electorate link|Southland}}.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=17 September 2025 |page=21 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

==History==
The electorate was established in 1866 when it separated from the [[Wallace (New Zealand electorate)|Wallace electorate]].{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=159}}

The first representative was [[William Wood (politician, born 1827)|William Wood]], who won the {{NZ election link|1866}}.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=149}} Wood retired at the end of the parliamentary term in 1870.  [[William Henderson Calder]] succeeded Wood in the {{NZ election link|1871}} and he resigned in March 1873.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=99}} The resulting {{By-election link|Invercargill|1873}} was won by [[John Cuthbertson (politician)|John Cuthbertson]], who served until the end of the parliamentary term in 1875.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=102}}

Cuthbertson was defeated by [[George Lumsden]] in the [[1875–1876 New Zealand general election|1875 election]]. Lumsden resigned in June 1878,{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=120}} which caused the {{By-election link|Invercargill|1878}}. [[Henry Feldwick]] was the successful candidate and he commenced his first of three terms for the electorate.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=105}} At the {{NZ election link|1879}}, Feldwick was defeated by [[James Walker Bain]], who retired at the end of the parliamentary term in 1881.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=94}} At the {{NZ election link|1881}}, Feldwick was again the successful candidate,{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=105}} only to be defeated again at the {{NZ election link|1884}}, on that occasion by [[Joseph Hatch]].{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=112}}  At the {{NZ election link|1887}}, Feldwick defeated Hatch and commenced his third and final term for the Invercargill electorate, serving until the end of the parliamentary term in 1890.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=105}}

[[James Whyte Kelly]] defeated Feldwick in the {{NZ election link|1890}}. Kelly became a member of the [[New Zealand Liberal Party|Liberal Party]] and served for three parliamentary terms,{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=105}} but broke away from the Liberal Party and became an [[Independent Liberal]] in 1895.<ref>{{cite news |title=The New Parliament |url= http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=OW18961210.2.81 |access-date=7 January 2014 |work=[[Otago Witness]] |date=10 December 1896 |issue=2232 |page=20}}</ref> For the {{NZ election link|1899}}, the [[Josiah Hanan]] of the Liberal Party challenged Kelly, with Hanan being successful.<ref>{{cite news |title=The Invercargill Seat |url= http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=TC18991212.2.12.3 |access-date=24 November 2011 |work=Colonist |date=12 December 1899 |volume=XLIII |issue=9659 |page=3}}</ref> Hanan served the electorate until 1925, when he retired.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=111}}

The {{NZ election link|1925}} was narrowly won by the former Prime Minister Sir [[Joseph Ward]] standing for the Liberal Party, who beat [[James Hargest]] of the [[Reform Party (New Zealand)|Reform Party]] with 4957 votes to 4798; a third contender, Patrick Hickey, stood for the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]].<ref name="1925 election" /> Until 1919, Ward had for many years represented Awarua.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=146}} Ward, a former leader of the Liberal Party, contested the election under the "Liberal" label, despite the fact that the remnants of the Liberal Party were now calling themselves by different names.<ref name="DNZB Ward">{{DNZB|Bassett|Michael|2W9|Ward, Joseph George 1856–1930|26 November 2011}}</ref> In 1928, Ward helped form the [[United Party (New Zealand)|United Party]]<ref name="DNZB Ward" /> and won the {{NZ election link|1928}}.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=146}} He died on 8 July 1930,<ref name="DNZB Ward" /> which caused the {{By-election link|Invercargill|1930}} won by his son [[Vincent Ward (politician)|Vincent Ward]], who retired at the end of the parliamentary term in 1931.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=146}}

Vincent Ward was succeeded by [[James Hargest]] in the {{NZ election link|1931}}. At the end of the parliamentary term in 1935, Hargest successfully contested the Awarua electorate.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=146}} He was succeeded in the Invercargill electorate by [[William Denham]] of the Labour Party, who held the electorate for three terms from 1935{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=103}} until his defeat in the {{NZ election link|1946}} by [[Ralph Hanan]] of the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]].{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=111}} Hanan was re-elected seven times and died in office on 24 July 1969; the need to hold a by-election before the [[1969 New Zealand general election|general election]] on 29 November was avoided by a special act, the [http://www.nzlii.org/nz/legis/hist_act/bpa19691969n35326/ By-election Postponement Act 1969].

The successful candidate in the 1969 general election was [[John Chewings]], who was defeated at the end of the parliamentary term at the {{NZ election link|1972}} by Labour's [[J. B. Munro]]. At the next [[1975 New Zealand general election|election in 1975]], Munro was in turn beaten by National's [[Norman Jones (politician)|Norman Jones]]. Jones retired at the end of his fourth term in August 1987 and died shortly thereafter on 19 November.<ref>{{cite web |title=Obituary—N. P. H. Jones |url=http://www.vdig.net/hansard/archive.jsp?y=1987&m=11&d=19&o=2&p=2 |publisher=VDIG group |access-date=25 November 2011 }}{{Dead link|date=November 2025 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref>

Jones was succeeded by National's [[Rob Munro (politician)|Rob Munro]] in the {{NZ election link|1987}}. Munro served two parliamentary terms before being beaten by Labour's [[Mark Peck]] in the {{NZ election link|1993}}. Peck retired after four parliamentary terms in 2005 and was succeeded by National's [[Eric Roy (politician)|Eric Roy]] in the {{NZ election link|2005}}. Roy retired after three parliamentary terms and was succeeded in {{NZ election link year|2014}} by [[Sarah Dowie]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Berwick |first1=Louise |last2=Mcdougall |first2=Nicci |last3=Mcleod |first3=Hannah |title=Soper won't stand again as Dowie wins city vote |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/southland-times/news/10525494/Soper-won-t-stand-again-as-Dowie-wins-city-vote |access-date=26 September 2014 |work=[[The Southland Times]] |date=20 September 2014}}</ref>

